1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,757
Table salt in water stays neutral.

2
00:00:02,757 --> 00:00:05,832
Washing soda in water
feels slippery and soapy.

3
00:00:05,832 --> 00:00:08,908
Ammonium chloride in
water turns a little sour.

4
00:00:09,308 --> 00:00:10,586
All three are salts.

5
00:00:10,586 --> 00:00:12,533
So why are they not all neutral?

6
00:00:12,983 --> 00:00:16,718
Diya dissolves washing soda in a
bucket at home to clean the floor.

7
00:00:16,718 --> 00:00:18,245
Her fingers feel slippery.

8
00:00:18,595 --> 00:00:20,072
Slippery means basic.

9
00:00:20,072 --> 00:00:21,719
But no base was added.

10
00:00:21,719 --> 00:00:22,748
Only a salt.

11
00:00:23,248 --> 00:00:27,558
By the end, you will predict the pH
of any salt, and see how one shared

12
00:00:27,558 --> 00:00:29,288
ion can calm down an acid.

13
00:00:29,938 --> 00:00:32,364
First, think of a busy playground swing.

14
00:00:32,714 --> 00:00:35,715
Children get on and off
the swing all the time.

15
00:00:35,715 --> 00:00:37,253
That is an equilibrium.

16
00:00:37,653 --> 00:00:40,500
Now a big group of the
same children rushes in.

17
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:43,703
The swing gets crowded, so
fewer new children get on.

18
00:00:44,103 --> 00:00:47,652
Add more of something that is already
there, and the equilibrium backs

19
00:00:47,652 --> 00:00:48,552
off.

20
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:50,317
You saw this with Le
Chatelier's principle.

21
00:00:50,917 --> 00:00:54,011
Let's put acetic acid
on our molecule bench.

22
00:00:54,361 --> 00:00:57,127
A few molecules break up
into hydronium and acetate.

23
00:00:57,127 --> 00:00:59,265
Alpha is about one point three percent.

24
00:00:59,665 --> 00:01:01,336
Now add sodium acetate.

25
00:01:01,336 --> 00:01:03,749
It brings a crowd of acetate ions.

26
00:01:03,749 --> 00:01:05,633
Acetate is the common ion.

27
00:01:05,633 --> 00:01:06,845
It is in both.

28
00:01:07,245 --> 00:01:10,634
Acetate grabs hydronium and
turns back into acetic acid.

29
00:01:10,634 --> 00:01:13,107
The equilibrium moves back to the left.

30
00:01:13,507 --> 00:01:17,997
With point one molar sodium acetate,
alpha falls to about point zero one

31
00:01:17,997 --> 00:01:19,058
seven percent.

32
00:01:19,058 --> 00:01:20,683
Seventy six times less.

33
00:01:20,683 --> 00:01:24,876
The pH rises from two point eight
eight to four point seven six.

34
00:01:25,476 --> 00:01:27,608
This is the common ion effect.

35
00:01:27,958 --> 00:01:32,979
Adding an ion that is already present
lowers the ionization of a weak acid

36
00:01:32,979 --> 00:01:34,335
or a weak base.

37
00:01:34,735 --> 00:01:40,309
In the same way, ammonium chloride lowers
the ionization of ammonium hydroxide.

38
00:01:40,309 --> 00:01:42,419
The common ion is ammonium.

39
00:01:43,069 --> 00:01:45,508
Now, why are some salts not neutral?

40
00:01:45,508 --> 00:01:47,359
Their ions react with water.

41
00:01:47,359 --> 00:01:49,058
This is called hydrolysis.

42
00:01:49,058 --> 00:01:50,084
Step by step.

43
00:01:50,434 --> 00:01:51,334
Step one.

44
00:01:51,069 --> 00:01:53,113
A salt comes from an acid and a base.

45
00:01:53,113 --> 00:01:54,661
Its ions are their leftovers.

46
00:01:55,411 --> 00:01:56,311
Step two.

47
00:01:56,222 --> 00:02:00,847
The leftover of a weak acid, like
acetate, takes a proton from water.

48
00:02:00,847 --> 00:02:02,359
That leaves hydroxide.

49
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:04,286
So the solution turns basic.

50
00:02:05,036 --> 00:02:05,936
Step three.

51
00:02:05,829 --> 00:02:09,822
The leftover of a weak base, like
ammonium, gives a proton to water.

52
00:02:09,822 --> 00:02:11,098
That makes hydronium.

53
00:02:11,098 --> 00:02:12,822
So the solution turns acidic.

54
00:02:13,572 --> 00:02:14,472
Step four.

55
00:02:14,302 --> 00:02:18,314
Leftovers of strong acids and bases,
like sodium and chloride, do not react

56
00:02:18,314 --> 00:02:19,480
with water at all.

57
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:22,081
So there are four types of salts.

58
00:02:22,431 --> 00:02:26,053
Strong acid and strong
base, like sodium chloride.

59
00:02:26,053 --> 00:02:27,217
No hydrolysis.

60
00:02:27,217 --> 00:02:28,117
pH seven.

61
00:02:28,509 --> 00:02:31,914
Weak acid and strong
base, like sodium acetate.

62
00:02:31,914 --> 00:02:32,814
Basic.

63
00:02:32,568 --> 00:02:36,069
pH equals seven plus
half of p K a plus log c.

64
00:02:36,469 --> 00:02:39,990
Strong acid and weak base,
like ammonium chloride.

65
00:02:39,990 --> 00:02:40,890
Acidic.

66
00:02:40,688 --> 00:02:44,198
pH equals seven minus
half of p K b plus log c.

67
00:02:44,598 --> 00:02:47,836
Weak acid and weak base,
like ammonium acetate.

68
00:02:47,836 --> 00:02:51,278
pH equals seven plus half
of p K a minus p K b.

69
00:02:51,278 --> 00:02:53,427
It depends on which is weaker.

70
00:02:54,027 --> 00:02:54,927
Compare.

71
00:02:54,693 --> 00:02:57,608
Sodium chloride: both
parents strong, neutral.

72
00:02:57,608 --> 00:03:01,367
Sodium acetate: the base parent
is strong, so it is basic.

73
00:03:01,767 --> 00:03:03,082
Pause and think.

74
00:03:03,082 --> 00:03:08,022
Washing soda is sodium carbonate, from
weak carbonic acid and strong sodium

75
00:03:08,022 --> 00:03:08,922
hydroxide.

76
00:03:08,888 --> 00:03:10,203
Acidic or basic?

77
00:03:13,403 --> 00:03:14,303
Basic.

78
00:03:13,891 --> 00:03:15,403
The stronger parent decides.

79
00:03:15,403 --> 00:03:17,539
That is why Diya's fingers felt slippery.

80
00:03:18,189 --> 00:03:19,089
Remember this.

81
00:03:18,969 --> 00:03:21,183
The stronger parent
decides the pH of a salt.

82
00:03:21,883 --> 00:03:23,573
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:23,873 --> 00:03:27,097
Find the pH of point one
molar sodium acetate.

84
00:03:27,097 --> 00:03:30,394
p K a of acetic acid is
four point seven six.

85
00:03:30,894 --> 00:03:33,549
It is a weak acid, strong base salt.

86
00:03:33,549 --> 00:03:36,850
pH equals seven plus
half of p K a plus log c.

87
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,957
Log of point one is minus one.

88
00:03:39,957 --> 00:03:45,137
So p K a plus log c is four point
seven six minus one, three point seven

89
00:03:45,137 --> 00:03:46,037
six.

90
00:03:46,441 --> 00:03:48,608
Half of that is one point eight eight.

91
00:03:48,608 --> 00:03:50,981
pH equals seven plus
one point eight eight.

92
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:52,374
Eight point eight eight.

93
00:03:53,124 --> 00:03:55,829
Above seven, so basic, as we predicted.

94
00:03:56,479 --> 00:03:57,940
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:03:58,290 --> 00:04:01,617
Students think every salt
solution is neutral, pH seven.

96
00:04:02,017 --> 00:04:06,197
Only salts of a strong acid
and a strong base are neutral.

97
00:04:06,197 --> 00:04:08,022
Sodium acetate is basic.

98
00:04:08,022 --> 00:04:10,063
Ammonium chloride is acidic.

99
00:04:10,713 --> 00:04:12,072
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:12,072 --> 00:04:16,020
Boards ask you to predict if a
salt is acidic, basic or neutral.

101
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:19,066
Entrance tests ask for the
pH using these formulas.

102
00:04:19,666 --> 00:04:20,904
Exam level now.

103
00:04:20,904 --> 00:04:25,666
Find the pH of point zero one molar
ammonium chloride at two ninety eight

104
00:04:25,666 --> 00:04:26,566
kelvin.

105
00:04:26,358 --> 00:04:29,452
p K b of ammonia is four point seven five.

106
00:04:29,452 --> 00:04:30,742
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:36,142 --> 00:04:37,492
The answer is B.

108
00:04:37,492 --> 00:04:39,479
About five point six three.

109
00:04:39,979 --> 00:04:42,725
It is a strong acid, weak base salt.

110
00:04:42,725 --> 00:04:46,192
pH equals seven minus
half of p K b plus log c.

111
00:04:46,692 --> 00:04:49,178
Log of point zero one is minus two.

112
00:04:49,178 --> 00:04:52,898
Four point seven five minus
two is two point seven five.

113
00:04:53,398 --> 00:04:55,807
Half is one point three seven five.

114
00:04:55,807 --> 00:04:58,574
Seven minus that is five point six three.

115
00:04:58,574 --> 00:05:00,167
A used the plus sign.

116
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:02,307
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:02,307 --> 00:05:03,890
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:07,090 --> 00:05:09,276
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:09,676 --> 00:05:10,576
One.

120
00:05:10,207 --> 00:05:14,220
Why does sodium acetate lower
the ionization of acetic acid?

121
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:15,920
Two.

122
00:05:15,568 --> 00:05:19,482
Is ammonium chloride solution
acidic or basic, and why?

123
00:05:20,282 --> 00:05:21,182
Three.

124
00:05:20,773 --> 00:05:22,843
Which salt type always gives pH seven?

125
00:05:23,743 --> 00:05:27,028
Next time: you run hard
and your muscles make acid.

126
00:05:27,028 --> 00:05:29,834
Yet your blood stays
at pH seven point four.

127
00:05:29,834 --> 00:05:30,734
How?

128
00:05:30,313 --> 00:05:32,387
We find out in Buffer Solutions.
